---
import CalcPage from '../../components/CalcPage.astro';
import Field from '../../components/Field.astro';
---

<CalcPage
  title="Percentage strength"
  kicker="Concentration"
  lede="A percentage is not really a number to plug into a formula — it is a conversion factor in disguise. Once you write it as one, these problems become unit cancellation and nothing more."
>
  <form slot="form" id="calc">
    <div class="field-grid">
      <div class="field field--wide">
        <label for="kind">Type of percentage strength</label>
        <select id="kind" name="kind">
          <option value="w/v">% w/v — grams in 100 mL (solutions)</option>
          <option value="w/w">% w/w — grams in 100 g (ointments, creams)</option>
          <option value="v/v">% v/v — mL in 100 mL (liquid in liquid)</option>
        </select>
        <span class="hint">
          Pick the one that matches how the preparation is described.
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="field-grid">
      <Field label="Percentage strength (%)" name="percent" placeholder="5" />
      <Field
        label="Amount of active ingredient"
        name="amount"
        placeholder="leave blank"
      />
      <Field label="Total quantity of preparation" name="total" placeholder="500" />
    </div>

    <p class="hint" style="margin-top:-0.6rem">
      Fill in two of the three boxes. Leave the one you want empty.
      Units are set automatically by the type you chose above.
    </p>

    <div class="field-actions">
      <button class="btn btn--primary" type="submit">Solve</button>
      <button class="btn btn--quiet" type="reset">Clear</button>
    </div>

    <div class="field-actions" style="margin-top:0.9rem">
      <button
        class="btn btn--quiet btn--tiny"
        type="button"
        data-example='{"kind":"w/v","percent":"5","amount":"","total":"500"}'
      >
        Example: D5W 500 mL
      </button>
      <button
        class="btn btn--quiet btn--tiny"
        type="button"
        data-example='{"kind":"w/w","percent":"","amount":"2","total":"60"}'
      >
        Example: ointment % w/w
      </button>
      <button
        class="btn btn--quiet btn--tiny"
        type="button"
        data-example='{"kind":"w/v","percent":"0.9","amount":"4.5","total":""}'
      >
        Example: normal saline volume
      </button>
    </div>
  </form>

  <div slot="concept">
    <h2>A percentage is a conversion factor</h2>
    <p>
      Percent means "per hundred". What the hundred is measuring is the only
      difference between the three types:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>% w/v</strong> — grams of ingredient in 100 mL of preparation.</li>
      <li><strong>% w/w</strong> — grams of ingredient in 100 g of preparation.</li>
      <li><strong>% v/v</strong> — milliliters of ingredient in 100 mL of preparation.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      So the moment you read "5% w/v", write it down as a fraction. That
      fraction is a true equality, which means you are allowed to multiply by
      it without changing anything:
    </p>

    <pre class="formula">  5 g
────────
 100 mL</pre>

    <p>
      Need the grams in 500 mL? Multiply, with milliliters on the bottom so
      they cancel:
    </p>

    <pre class="formula">          5 g
500 mL × ──────  =  25 g
         100 mL</pre>

    <h3>Going the other way, flip the factor</h3>
    <p>
      If you know the grams and want the volume, use the same factor upside
      down so that grams are the thing that cancels:
    </p>

    <pre class="formula">          100 mL
4.5 g × ─────────  =  500 mL
          0.9 g</pre>

    <p>
      There is no separate rule for "solving backwards". You are always
      multiplying by the same factor — the only decision is which way up it
      goes, and the unit you want to get rid of tells you.
    </p>

    <h3>Finding the percentage itself</h3>
    <p>
      If the percentage is what's missing, ask the question percent is already
      asking: <em>how much ingredient is in 100 of preparation?</em> Start with
      100 and cancel down. Whatever survives is the percentage.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        <strong>The shortcut worth memorizing:</strong> for % w/v, multiply the
        percentage by 10 to get mg/mL. So 0.9% w/v is 9 mg/mL, and 5% w/v is
        50 mg/mL. It saves a step constantly.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</CalcPage>

<script>
  import { setupCalculator } from '../../scripts/calc.js';
  import { solvePercentageStrength } from '../../scripts/solvers/percentage-strength.js';

  setupCalculator({
    form: '#calc',
    solve: solvePercentageStrength,
  });
</script>
